WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.800
Enter the world of fantasy, mystery, and excitement with Mickey and Minnie as you solve the mystery of Mickey's missing friend.

00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:10.640
This is Mickey Mousecapade.

00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:33.280
And welcome to NEStalgia, chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:35.260
I'm Mike. I'm Sean.

00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:36.760
I'm Joe.

00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:41.580
And did you guys catch that interesting bit of Disney trivia there?

00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:45.600
Alice is only friends with Mickey Mouse, not Minnie.

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:48.880
Yeah, Minnie and Alice famously don't get along.

00:00:49.240 --> 00:00:51.680
Now, are Mickey and Minnie like a couple?

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:53.560
I can never really remember that.

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:55.880
Or are they just really close friends?

00:00:56.260 --> 00:00:59.460
They're a couple. Okay, so yeah, I mean, I get it.

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:07.380
I mean, she would be very angry if, you know, if the guy has more gal friends than she has gal friends.

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:09.880
I mean, you've been in that situation before, right?

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:13.340
That human girl he's always hanging out with.

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:19.300
Yeah. Of course, you know, there's also more important things to talk about than just Mickey's friendship with...

00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:20.820
I prefer to talk about this.

00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:21.820
Yeah, all right.

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:30.060
That's good. You know, because this is also the start of Capcom and Disney and their little video game romance.

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:39.060
I don't know if you guys know about this, but there's like a bunch of other Disney Capcom games on the NES, but also on the Super Nintendo and the Sega Genesis and stuff like that.

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:43.000
You guys have any experience with any of those other ones before we start talking about Mickey Mouse campaign?

00:01:43.640 --> 00:01:45.060
I don't think so.

00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:48.540
Yeah, I owned the Great Circus Mystery.

00:01:49.100 --> 00:01:51.480
Okay. Which was a Super NES game.

00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:59.760
And like when I actually, because of this episode, I went and watched some footage from it and it was a blast from the past.

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:07.380
I remembered most of what was going on, including some of like the boss designs, but I was like five or six years old at that point.

00:02:08.460 --> 00:02:15.380
And I guess I didn't realize how easy it was because I guess one of the main complaints from the game was that it was easy.

00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:17.100
But I guess it's also for kids.

00:02:17.320 --> 00:02:19.880
As pretty much all Mickey Mouse games should be, right?

00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:21.260
I would hope so.

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:25.100
You know, can we talk about Mickey Mouse for a second though?

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:37.740
Because the mouse himself hasn't gotten like a ton of love in the last like 30 years or so outside of like some things like Kingdom Hearts and, you know, Disney World like has Mickey Mouse everywhere.

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:41.120
But he doesn't get like, there's no movies about Mickey Mouse.

00:02:41.300 --> 00:02:47.020
I think there was like a House of Mouse TV show on Disney Channel, but like even that's now a while ago.

00:02:47.220 --> 00:02:49.940
What do you guys think is up between Disney and the mouse?

00:02:49.940 --> 00:02:53.640
I felt like when I was, you know, growing up and everything, I really did.

00:02:53.760 --> 00:03:02.200
I mean, I knew of Mickey Mouse as this mascot, as this like, this, you know, ubiquitous character that's everywhere.

00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:05.040
But I don't think I ever knew anything about like his character.

00:03:05.300 --> 00:03:07.420
I don't think I had ever seen him in anything actually.

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:10.220
Like it's not like I had ever seen Steamboat Willie or any of that.

00:03:10.220 --> 00:03:22.680
I feel like he just kind of like, he like transcended like actual story and just became like the face of Disney for a while, at least in at least in my experience.

00:03:22.780 --> 00:03:25.580
And then I know like they have things more recently like Epic Mickey and stuff.

00:03:25.700 --> 00:03:32.140
It's the only thing I think of that like, that I would have been interested in that was like strictly Mickey Mouse based.

00:03:32.140 --> 00:03:34.120
I mean, not a clinic in parts.

00:03:34.820 --> 00:03:44.100
Yeah, I think I agree with Joe here where it just sort of turned into Mickey Mouse as just a manifestation of the company now.

00:03:44.100 --> 00:04:02.040
Like he's not in anything, but they're going to use the ears as, as like a, as an icon from now until the end of time, even though there won't be any actual like film or TV with him in it for another hundred years.

00:04:03.380 --> 00:04:09.960
Yeah, I'd argue even that like making a Mickey Mouse movie would be a mistake on Disney's part.

00:04:09.960 --> 00:04:19.820
Like at this point, don't do anything with him because it would probably be uninteresting or uninspiring and diminish the character at this point.

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:24.300
I think Mickey Mouse is kind of, you know, he's Walt Disney just now as a mouse.

00:04:24.760 --> 00:04:35.000
And another reason why you don't really want to use Mickey Mouse that much is because then you wind up with video games like this, which, you know, totally not conflicting my viewpoints here.

00:04:35.140 --> 00:04:37.580
But let's talk about Mickey Mouse Capade.

00:04:37.580 --> 00:04:45.240
It's a, it's a platforming game where you control, somehow control both Mickey and Minnie through about four or five stages.

00:04:45.380 --> 00:04:55.120
And you throw these shooting stars at various enemies from all different types of Disney games and Disney movies rather.

00:04:55.460 --> 00:04:59.180
And they're all doing this to find Alice from Alice in Wonderland.

00:04:59.180 --> 00:05:03.340
And I suppose to rescue her from her nap.

00:05:04.360 --> 00:05:10.300
But like, I guess that would make Mickey trapped inside her dream, but we're not going to get into that.

00:05:10.520 --> 00:05:12.460
Let's just talk about the game overall.

00:05:13.100 --> 00:05:14.400
Sean, what were your feelings on it?

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:24.640
My first thought was that this game sort of controls like Ice Climbers and Smash Brothers, but not as intuitively.

00:05:25.600 --> 00:05:31.540
Because it's not like one will automatically, because you're controlling both Mickey and me at the same time.

00:05:31.700 --> 00:05:36.160
And it's not like one will automatically do the exact same movement.

00:05:36.160 --> 00:05:42.460
Like, you're just controlling both of them from, like, the positions that they're in.

00:05:42.660 --> 00:05:46.780
So you can really mess yourself up because of that.

00:05:47.480 --> 00:05:49.000
That was my first thought.

00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:53.960
And my second was that I really hated the fact that the music just kept repeating.

00:05:55.860 --> 00:06:04.380
Yeah, that's a huge problem in these NES games when they just have, like, one track on the jukebox and they just keep replaying them over and over and over again.

00:06:04.920 --> 00:06:08.620
To go back to your Mickey and Minnie thought there, because I think that is important.

00:06:09.160 --> 00:06:12.040
It's also important that they didn't just make them 1-1, though, right?

00:06:12.100 --> 00:06:16.860
Because otherwise they'd just be on top of each other at all times and there'd be no reason to have Minnie there.

00:06:17.380 --> 00:06:18.760
But they could have done it better.

00:06:18.760 --> 00:06:27.820
Yeah, one thing that we played recently that sort of had multiple characters being controlled by one input was Ghostbusters.

00:06:28.120 --> 00:06:31.280
I don't think that they should have gone that route either.

00:06:31.540 --> 00:06:42.420
But in that, it was just they would, each character would mimic your input, but somewhat delayed because they're behind you, if you know what I mean.

00:06:42.800 --> 00:06:49.880
Yep. So I don't know which way would have been better if you just got rid of the press A to step forward button.

00:06:50.820 --> 00:06:54.580
But I mean, it wasn't like this ruined the game.

00:06:54.720 --> 00:06:57.960
It was just an oddity that I felt was interesting.

00:06:58.200 --> 00:07:01.580
Yeah, it was weird because I feel like there are a couple ways you can do it.

00:07:01.620 --> 00:07:07.460
And I don't think it's necessarily like a complete no-no to do it where it's one-to-one exactly.

00:07:07.460 --> 00:07:13.860
You just have to have the character that's behind be like a second delayed so that they're never literally right on top of each other.

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:16.760
Kind of like if you played like Final Fantasy VIII.

00:07:17.000 --> 00:07:18.480
Yeah, that's my other thought.

00:07:18.680 --> 00:07:19.760
Exactly the same.

00:07:21.240 --> 00:07:33.880
Granted, that makes the gameplay a little different where I think that the fact that they're not stuck together completely made some, like you said, Sean, some annoying things possible that I definitely do not think were intentional.

00:07:33.880 --> 00:07:37.880
And also some interesting, like helpful things that I still don't think were intentional.

00:07:38.180 --> 00:07:40.840
I think there's some ways you can kind of cheese this game because of that.

00:07:41.060 --> 00:07:45.660
Yeah. Like you can get Minnie stuck somewhere while Mickey is safe and Minnie cannot be hit.

00:07:46.180 --> 00:07:48.420
So then you're just kind of safe.

00:07:48.600 --> 00:07:51.880
But then on the other hand, you might just be focusing on Mickey for a minute.

00:07:52.080 --> 00:07:53.500
You're right at the spot where you need to leave.

00:07:53.640 --> 00:08:01.120
You're running away from an enemy and then you can't leave because you realize Minnie is stuck three levels below you, like stuck on like a wall or something.

00:08:01.900 --> 00:08:03.940
So I don't know if any of that was intentional.

00:08:04.160 --> 00:08:09.200
But if it was, I think they could have designed some interesting mechanics around it.

00:08:09.540 --> 00:08:15.260
Yeah. I mean, also the laws of Minnie throughout the game do change quite a bit.

00:08:15.440 --> 00:08:19.600
Like she can't have any damage done to her by enemies.

00:08:19.600 --> 00:08:21.200
So she just can walk right through them.

00:08:21.200 --> 00:08:33.920
But since you control Mickey primarily, if you successfully jump over a platform but Minnie doesn't, if she goes into pitfall, that's a game over for both characters.

00:08:34.180 --> 00:08:41.920
So it is a little strange to have Minnie act like the ice climbers from Smash Brothers if we're going to use that analogy.

00:08:41.920 --> 00:08:42.920
I do think it's very clear.

00:08:43.060 --> 00:08:44.240
I think that helps paint a picture.

00:08:44.240 --> 00:08:51.380
If we're going to use that example, what would have been better off is Minnie just also like respawning later on somewhere else.

00:08:51.580 --> 00:08:53.720
You know, like if she falls, it's no big deal.

00:08:54.980 --> 00:09:02.020
But also like, you know, if she's going to be – if she's going to fall and that's going to end the game, she should also be responsible for taking damage as well.

00:09:02.020 --> 00:09:06.900
Because she can shoot stars of her own that Mickey can't.

00:09:07.040 --> 00:09:15.100
So if they're both able to shoot stars and they're both able to, you know, traverse, they should be also accountable as the same thing, no?

00:09:15.520 --> 00:09:17.360
I mean, I agree.

00:09:17.360 --> 00:09:27.680
Like in principle, I would – I think that that would be a much more – a much worse experience because we would be getting hit a lot more.

00:09:28.760 --> 00:09:36.960
Yeah. Right. They would have had to design the game with the idea of like Mickey and Minnie working in tandem on separate parts of the stage, right?

00:09:37.080 --> 00:09:38.340
Yeah. If they wanted to do it that way.

00:09:38.940 --> 00:09:51.320
Yeah. I think what you mentioned about Minnie falling and that suddenly counting against you bothered me a lot because, first of all, the entire first level, which is pretty long, nothing can do damage to Minnie.

00:09:51.480 --> 00:09:56.240
So you're already kind of conditioned to like, okay, she can fire, but she doesn't get hit by things.

00:09:56.340 --> 00:09:57.200
You don't have to worry about her.

00:09:57.700 --> 00:10:00.680
Now, by this point, it's like – there's just like too much to think about.

00:10:00.860 --> 00:10:04.740
Like, okay, so you got to focus on Minnie when things are coming at – or Mickey when things are coming at you.

00:10:04.860 --> 00:10:07.860
Don't worry about Minnie. But when you're jumping, you have to worry about both of them.

00:10:07.960 --> 00:10:12.000
And like let's be honest, like at a quick glance, it's hard to tell which one's which.

00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:17.480
Like Minnie has a bow and like five pixels of a pink shirt where like Mickey doesn't.

00:10:17.700 --> 00:10:21.560
But like otherwise, like they're just – it's pretty easy to confuse them.

00:10:21.720 --> 00:10:28.880
And I feel like in the levels where you have to look at both of them, it gets a little confusing because I'll be like, wait, which one's in front?

00:10:29.160 --> 00:10:33.080
You know, because the one in front is the one that you're always like primarily like controlling.

00:10:33.780 --> 00:10:38.420
Yeah. Like in the first level where you're – I think it's the fun house.

00:10:38.900 --> 00:10:45.160
Yeah. Where you just sort of traverse through the same room over and over with different colored walls.

00:10:46.200 --> 00:10:48.960
I noticed that, oh, she fell down the ladder.

00:10:49.200 --> 00:10:50.140
And like, oh, that's weird.

00:10:50.480 --> 00:10:56.680
That's when I noticed that she's also like a physical character in the world and not just sort of mimicking your actions.

00:10:58.400 --> 00:10:59.680
And like, but that's okay.

00:11:00.020 --> 00:11:00.840
I finished the level.

00:11:01.020 --> 00:11:02.040
That level was pretty easy.

00:11:02.180 --> 00:11:09.020
And then we get to the ocean and they've got these like tidal waves that come for you.

00:11:09.160 --> 00:11:18.400
And if you try and sort of do some weird movements in the air to just like delay your fall so you don't get hit by a tidal wave,

00:11:18.580 --> 00:11:21.740
she will fall between and end your run right there.

00:11:23.320 --> 00:11:24.360
It's odd.

00:11:25.700 --> 00:11:31.300
I guess that's just what my – how my opinion on it morphed over two levels.

00:11:31.940 --> 00:11:41.920
Yeah, no. We should talk about the platforming aspect of the game because it isn't opposed to using mechanics that you might see in a Super Mario Brothers style game

00:11:41.920 --> 00:11:49.820
where you have to jump from platform to another ledge to even carrying momentum from one section of the stage to another.

00:11:49.820 --> 00:11:56.880
Later on in the game in like I think the castle stage, there's like specific elements of platforming where there are different ledges.

00:11:57.060 --> 00:12:06.100
And it's actually I think purposeful to design a challenge where you have to make sure that both Mickey and Minnie successfully land on each of these ledges.

00:12:06.100 --> 00:12:10.300
Because if you get to the end with just Mickey, you can't progress to the next stage.

00:12:10.320 --> 00:12:14.020
You would have to go back down to the beginning and work through it again as a team.

00:12:14.020 --> 00:12:16.720
So I definitely think like some of this stuff is purposeful.

00:12:16.800 --> 00:12:25.020
But then back to that level you were talking about, Sean, the ocean level, the second stage, there's very little intent on the platforming there.

00:12:25.220 --> 00:12:27.500
I don't know if you guys have ever played Super Mario World.

00:12:27.640 --> 00:12:28.920
It's my favorite Mario game.

00:12:29.180 --> 00:12:41.560
Of course. But there's a level in that game early on where it's a water-based level and a bunch of dolphins like run into you and you can ride the top of them.

00:12:41.700 --> 00:12:43.760
But you're also trying to like actively avoid them.

00:12:43.860 --> 00:12:45.120
And it all makes sense.

00:12:45.240 --> 00:12:46.360
Like they're purposefully placed.

00:12:46.520 --> 00:12:51.140
It's easy to jump over one or land on another one to help it carry you along.

00:12:51.340 --> 00:12:53.140
Like it all checks out.

00:12:53.140 --> 00:12:58.020
In this level instead, birds just fly from any particular direction.

00:12:58.380 --> 00:13:05.320
If you dodge the first wave, there's an automatic second wave right behind it that is guaranteed to hit you.

00:13:05.940 --> 00:13:16.580
You know, like the jump mechanic doesn't allow for anything other than like close to frame perfect inputs to make sure that you get over both of those waves.

00:13:16.580 --> 00:13:23.560
And so you're pretty much just damage rolling through this stage to make sure that you continue to progress along.

00:13:23.680 --> 00:13:26.560
Because otherwise, you're just going to keep getting hit by the waves that keep coming.

00:13:26.780 --> 00:13:34.020
So what I notice, and usually when we talk about a level design like this, the culprit usually is that they just have –

00:13:34.460 --> 00:13:40.720
it's just like as randomly spawning enemies just so that there's something on the screen for you to have to dodge or move around or kill.

00:13:40.720 --> 00:13:51.980
So, but I had to play this level so often because I kept dying from the boss too was I would take it very slowly.

00:13:52.240 --> 00:13:59.120
And I noticed that if I was slow, the enemies weren't spawning on the same screen.

00:13:59.120 --> 00:14:05.020
So it's somehow worse to me because it means that these were hand placed poorly.

00:14:05.460 --> 00:14:08.620
Not just like, oh, it's just random and it's bad because it's random.

00:14:09.020 --> 00:14:13.800
It was just bad placement because they put them there themselves.

00:14:14.980 --> 00:14:19.160
Yeah, it's like they thought like what would be hard – oh, it would be hard if this and this and this were here.

00:14:19.240 --> 00:14:24.380
They didn't think about spacing them out or putting them in a way where it's a fun game to try – you know what I mean?

00:14:24.480 --> 00:14:28.680
Like it's hard to articulate I guess what we mean exactly.

00:14:28.680 --> 00:14:39.080
No, I think it makes sense though, Joe, because it's – the difference is that there are times in this game where things are done really –

00:14:39.560 --> 00:14:42.000
you know, they're done well and they're done with intention.

00:14:42.560 --> 00:14:49.420
And so therefore it kind of seems to me like the whole game was made with details in mind.

00:14:49.420 --> 00:14:56.680
But it just seems like at other times the details that they include are just too punishing for the game that they're playing.

00:14:56.680 --> 00:15:09.360
You know, like it doesn't make any sense to have to program the two waves to go back to back or later on to have like certain enemies immediately as you enter a screen that they're just on top of you, you know?

00:15:09.540 --> 00:15:17.500
Right. Yeah, it's like they didn't put thought into like, okay, what would be the best timing for these to come up to make this like an experience that feels challenging but attainable?

00:15:17.940 --> 00:15:24.860
Not like just, okay, this is just a mess and I got to kind of like spray and pray and hope that I hit what I get, what I'm going for.

00:15:24.860 --> 00:15:29.500
You know, and then there's also the shooting star, our only weapon in the game.

00:15:29.920 --> 00:15:35.400
And this is a super annoying complaint from me, but I do think it's valid.

00:15:35.620 --> 00:15:38.420
It's the only weapon in the game.

00:15:38.460 --> 00:15:40.760
So why do you have to find it first?

00:15:40.960 --> 00:15:46.460
Like what is that all about with going to the treasure chest and finding the shooting star?

00:15:46.580 --> 00:15:48.480
Like you should just have that available to you.

00:15:48.640 --> 00:15:53.120
It's like Mickey never learned the powers of shooting stars until he found it inside that one chest.

00:15:53.120 --> 00:15:54.120
Well, here's the thing, Mike.

00:15:54.340 --> 00:16:01.860
Mike, they were – Capcom was bound by the Disney incorporated deep lore.

00:16:02.220 --> 00:16:09.200
And because in previous iterations or previous movies, Mickey never had a shooting star power.

00:16:09.300 --> 00:16:12.000
So he had to go and get it first.

00:16:12.120 --> 00:16:13.580
I hope you understand.

00:16:14.420 --> 00:16:17.000
Now, I would argue that that's not true though, right?

00:16:17.180 --> 00:16:29.000
Because didn't he like in the VHS home videos of all the Disney movies, didn't they start with Mickey as the Sorcerer's Apprentice like shooting a star out of his hand?

00:16:30.120 --> 00:16:31.700
I think so.

00:16:32.640 --> 00:16:34.740
So he clearly had this power.

00:16:34.920 --> 00:16:35.920
Which one inspired which?

00:16:35.920 --> 00:16:38.860
I don't know. Were the VHS is inspired by the NES game?

00:16:38.860 --> 00:16:39.680
You tell me.

00:16:40.060 --> 00:16:42.420
It's not the actual movie.

00:16:42.560 --> 00:16:43.700
It's just on the VHS.

00:16:46.580 --> 00:16:50.560
Now, I agree with you, Mike, that I think it's weird.

00:16:50.840 --> 00:16:51.960
That was a weird decision.

00:16:52.280 --> 00:16:56.000
But to play devil's advocate, I think I can see the argument for it.

00:16:56.160 --> 00:16:59.800
I don't think it necessarily works in the way that they did it.

00:16:59.800 --> 00:17:10.900
But I guess there is like something to having to get through like a portion of this level where you don't have a weapon to then see how that changes when you do have it.

00:17:11.120 --> 00:17:24.380
You know what I mean? Like I'm thinking of Pokemon and when they first introduced like running shoes into Pokemon, they would make you walk for the beginning of it for the first, you know, 20 minutes, half hour of the game and then get the running shoes.

00:17:24.520 --> 00:17:27.940
So you can be like, oh, wow, these running shoes are great because they make the game easier.

00:17:27.940 --> 00:17:33.860
Like I said, I don't think it necessarily accomplishes that, like something that needed to be accomplished.

00:17:34.060 --> 00:17:36.660
But I can see that being like an argument.

00:17:36.900 --> 00:17:44.020
It's a kind of tool that you see in much more, much deeper games.

00:17:45.380 --> 00:17:54.940
I guess it's sort of like a film student putting like symbolism into their like student film.

00:17:54.940 --> 00:17:59.620
Like the game, like the student film doesn't require this symbolism.

00:17:59.960 --> 00:18:02.820
Yeah. And this game doesn't require that technique.

00:18:03.020 --> 00:18:03.920
Do you kind of know what I'm saying?

00:18:04.180 --> 00:18:10.220
Totally. Yeah. It's like there's not enough to this game to be like, wow, this is going to make it so much more.

00:18:10.540 --> 00:18:12.840
People are going to appreciate the stars so much now.

00:18:12.840 --> 00:18:25.840
Yeah. The argument there, of course, is that we've already been trained on NES games like Metroid to believe that, you know, it's OK to learn power ups and, you know, acquire these things as you go through.

00:18:25.840 --> 00:18:33.580
But you need like you need more treasure chests with more active like shooting power or something throughout the game as well.

00:18:33.700 --> 00:18:35.080
And this isn't a Metroidvania.

00:18:35.300 --> 00:18:36.600
Nothing else happens.

00:18:36.740 --> 00:18:38.780
You just find the shooting star.

00:18:38.940 --> 00:18:41.440
And then it's like, I hope you enjoy the one power up.

00:18:41.440 --> 00:18:43.640
The first level is pretty Metroidvania.

00:18:45.060 --> 00:18:54.800
Well, you know, it's funny because you can, even though Joe made it seem like, you know, you learn how to play the game without the shooting star first and then you get the shooting star.

00:18:54.980 --> 00:19:03.900
You can just not notice that that door is there and you can just continue on with the rest of the level, never getting the shooting star and make it all the way to the boss.

00:19:04.440 --> 00:19:09.840
Obviously, not able to defeat the it's like a witch or something like that, not being able to defeat her.

00:19:10.060 --> 00:19:11.900
And then you have to then re-traverse.

00:19:12.140 --> 00:19:14.720
It's actually better at that point to just reset the game.

00:19:14.860 --> 00:19:20.660
But you could decide to like re-traverse all the way back to the beginning and go through that door you missed.

00:19:20.660 --> 00:19:32.440
Yeah, I think that they must have realized that kind of that oversight or that kind of mistake before they released the game because the manual clearly is like, do this, this, and this to get the star.

00:19:32.560 --> 00:19:34.000
First thing you should do is just go here, here.

00:19:34.260 --> 00:19:38.520
It like walks you through where to get the star because it's like, you should just start with the star.

00:19:38.700 --> 00:19:40.720
They panicked. They realized it after the fact.

00:19:40.960 --> 00:19:42.240
I was going to mention that.

00:19:42.240 --> 00:19:46.440
Like the manual is like half, half strategy guide for the first level.

00:19:48.740 --> 00:19:56.020
And it's like when you use these hints, like quote unquote hints, it's more just stuff we forgot to communicate to you through gameplay.

00:20:03.660 --> 00:20:07.800
You know, are there any enemies in this game that aren't annoying?

00:20:07.800 --> 00:20:14.140
And I don't mean like just from the way that they like populate the screen or the way they attack you.

00:20:14.320 --> 00:20:23.940
But I genuinely didn't find any enemy in this game fun, approachable, you know, cute in character design.

00:20:24.100 --> 00:20:25.540
It's just, it all just exists.

00:20:26.000 --> 00:20:30.500
I have one. Yeah. The blue eels are, they don't really do anything to annoy you.

00:20:30.600 --> 00:20:35.980
They just sort of scurry about and they're not super fast like those little dog things.

00:20:35.980 --> 00:20:39.760
And they don't bounce around like those blue bouncy balls.

00:20:40.000 --> 00:20:45.740
And they don't fall within the same frame like those chandeliers.

00:20:45.940 --> 00:20:47.220
It's, they just sort of exist.

00:20:47.420 --> 00:20:49.020
The chandelier is the worst.

00:20:50.300 --> 00:20:55.980
Yeah. Especially because sometimes the first time I experienced the chandelier, it was before one had fallen on me.

00:20:56.080 --> 00:20:58.700
So it was just a part of the background and I just jumped.

00:20:58.720 --> 00:21:01.420
It didn't fall. I just jumped and hit the chandelier and died.

00:21:01.700 --> 00:21:03.620
And I didn't even know that anything was there.

00:21:03.720 --> 00:21:05.400
I just thought like I've seen those chandeliers before.

00:21:05.400 --> 00:21:06.440
I didn't know it was an enemy.

00:21:06.720 --> 00:21:09.540
The first, the first time I saw a chandelier, one fell on me.

00:21:09.660 --> 00:21:12.380
So I was afraid of every chandelier from there on out.

00:21:13.920 --> 00:21:18.900
As far as another enemy that that was, I mean, I wouldn't, interesting as a, maybe an overstatement,

00:21:18.980 --> 00:21:22.920
but that was like remotely like recognizable, at least where the brooms.

00:21:23.200 --> 00:21:26.800
Yeah. And I just found that interesting because it was the only thing of the, of the main enemies

00:21:26.800 --> 00:21:28.560
that I was like, oh, that's a Disney thing.

00:21:28.940 --> 00:21:31.360
Yeah. The rest were just weird shapes.

00:21:31.360 --> 00:21:32.680
Shapes. Yeah.

00:21:32.840 --> 00:21:36.440
And less like our Disney knowledge is like really deep Disney knowledge that, that we

00:21:36.440 --> 00:21:43.360
don't have. But, uh, it just felt weird that there's like, oh, this one enemy that's, that's a recognizable

00:21:43.360 --> 00:21:45.020
Disney broom.

00:21:45.200 --> 00:21:46.360
And then there's all these other things.

00:21:46.840 --> 00:21:49.580
Are there a lot of pooping birds in Disney movies?

00:21:50.520 --> 00:21:52.800
Well, I think those are just projectiles, right?

00:21:52.980 --> 00:21:55.560
Like it's not, it's not necessarily shit.

00:21:55.780 --> 00:21:56.800
It looks like shit.

00:21:57.540 --> 00:22:01.740
All right. Well, you know, it's funny because even what Joe was talking about there is like, you might

00:22:01.740 --> 00:22:03.240
need some deep Disney lore.

00:22:03.580 --> 00:22:11.760
Uh, you kind of do because like, did the people who made this game ever watch any Disney movies?

00:22:11.760 --> 00:22:15.300
Like what is, there's no coherency to what's going on here.

00:22:15.300 --> 00:22:20.840
I mean, if we could just jump ahead to the end here for a second, the final boss is Maleficent.

00:22:21.000 --> 00:22:25.540
Now, why would Melissa Maleficent have Alice from Alice in Wonderland captive?

00:22:25.760 --> 00:22:26.820
That's not even the same game.

00:22:27.200 --> 00:22:28.780
That's not the last time that happens.

00:22:29.860 --> 00:22:30.760
Yeah, that's true.

00:22:30.920 --> 00:22:35.180
Uh, that maybe she was trying to just gather the seven princesses of light, you know?

00:22:36.060 --> 00:22:38.280
I guess that maybe they hadn't.

00:22:38.280 --> 00:22:40.180
Maybe it was, we have all these assets.

00:22:40.180 --> 00:22:45.580
We have the rights to these characters because we've given, we've been given carte blanche

00:22:45.580 --> 00:22:49.820
by Disney. Um, and they're like, oh, Maleficent looks cool.

00:22:50.020 --> 00:22:56.280
And you know, Alice in Wonderland sort of looks like a, like a damsel in distress sort of thing.

00:22:56.480 --> 00:23:02.980
So they just cobbled together the, the, the best like super Mario, like knockoff they could.

00:23:03.120 --> 00:23:05.760
I don't know. I can't say, I'm talking about Capcom.

00:23:05.760 --> 00:23:10.360
So this feels really, uh, this feels really bad, but I don't know.

00:23:10.560 --> 00:23:11.880
I can't, I can't tell you.

00:23:12.520 --> 00:23:14.380
Are you ready for the bad news?

00:23:14.760 --> 00:23:22.260
Okay. A Famicom version of this very game exists and has all of the correct enemies and bosses.

00:23:22.780 --> 00:23:26.900
So you're playing against Mickey specific enemies.

00:23:26.900 --> 00:23:33.360
So in the first stage, as I already alluded to, there's a witch in, at the end of the,

00:23:33.360 --> 00:23:35.720
like to be the boss of the fun house.

00:23:35.880 --> 00:23:39.740
And apparently, you know, here's some deep Disney lore for you.

00:23:40.220 --> 00:23:46.480
That witch is called Witch Hazel, a Donald Duck character, like exclusive to Donald Duck cartoons.

00:23:46.900 --> 00:23:49.060
That's what you get in the American version.

00:23:49.060 --> 00:23:55.120
But in the Famicom version, it's the Cheshire Cat from Alice in Wonderland.

00:23:55.200 --> 00:23:56.560
That kind of makes sense, right?

00:23:57.300 --> 00:23:58.100
I guess.

00:23:58.940 --> 00:24:01.920
Yeah. Right. That you would fight the enemies from Alice in Wonderland.

00:24:02.260 --> 00:24:04.300
Now. But was the Cheshire Cat an enemy, you know?

00:24:04.400 --> 00:24:05.760
I never saw Alice in Wonderland. He was deceiving.

00:24:05.840 --> 00:24:06.840
Wasn't he like a helpful?

00:24:06.920 --> 00:24:07.780
He smiled a lot.

00:24:07.800 --> 00:24:08.800
He was deceiving. Yeah.

00:24:10.080 --> 00:24:14.200
And then, and then the final boss, instead of being Maleficent, is the Queen of Hearts.

00:24:14.400 --> 00:24:15.600
Now, why change that?

00:24:15.600 --> 00:24:17.300
If it's in the Famicom version.

00:24:17.600 --> 00:24:18.580
But who's the Queen of Hearts?

00:24:19.000 --> 00:24:24.380
The Queen of Hearts is the bad woman who locks, who like sentences Alice to trial.

00:24:24.380 --> 00:24:29.100
So this is an Alice in Wonderland deep lore game with Mickey just in it.

00:24:29.620 --> 00:24:32.960
Mickey's in it because Mickey is, like I said, Walt Disney.

00:24:33.220 --> 00:24:35.500
He's just Walt Disney trying to rescue Alice.

00:24:35.740 --> 00:24:37.060
His IP. Yeah, his IP.

00:24:37.060 --> 00:24:39.640
From the public domain.

00:24:41.900 --> 00:24:45.340
Okay. I really appreciate this reading of it.

00:24:45.460 --> 00:24:46.760
I like this much better.

00:24:47.480 --> 00:24:54.060
Okay. Now, the game overall for the Famicom version obviously focuses more on Alice in Wonderland.

00:24:54.240 --> 00:24:59.180
But it also includes Captain Hook as a boss on the, like, ship level.

00:24:59.680 --> 00:25:01.240
Now. With Robin Williams?

00:25:01.260 --> 00:25:03.340
Which is weird. No, no, no.

00:25:03.400 --> 00:25:04.920
Not Hook. Captain Hook.

00:25:05.140 --> 00:25:12.340
Oh. Yeah, yeah, yeah. What's weird about this is that in the American version, there's no Captain Hook.

00:25:12.540 --> 00:25:18.860
And I just think that's baffling because the box art for Mickey Mousecapade in North America

00:25:18.860 --> 00:25:21.800
has the crocodile from Peter Pan right on the box.

00:25:21.860 --> 00:25:27.900
So if you're including, like, the idea that Peter Pan would be in this realm, you know,

00:25:27.980 --> 00:25:29.040
you don't have Peter Pan in the game.

00:25:29.160 --> 00:25:30.500
You don't have Captain Hook in the game.

00:25:30.520 --> 00:25:32.320
I don't remember seeing the crocodile.

00:25:32.500 --> 00:25:33.600
I saw the crocodile. But it's on the box.

00:25:34.260 --> 00:25:36.540
Okay. He's on the box, so he better be in the game.

00:25:36.700 --> 00:25:38.220
Yeah. But why remove Captain Hook?

00:25:38.500 --> 00:25:42.380
There can only be one captain in this game, and that's Captain Commando.

00:25:44.460 --> 00:25:46.680
Wasn't the crocodile the boss of the second level?

00:25:47.060 --> 00:25:48.060
Yeah. Oh, yes. That's right.

00:25:48.640 --> 00:25:50.960
Okay. No, I was thinking about the ship stage.

00:25:51.100 --> 00:25:52.140
Like, why wouldn't he be there?

00:25:52.140 --> 00:25:57.360
But I guess if you're removing Captain Hook, why have the crocodile show up in what's supposed

00:25:57.360 --> 00:26:01.000
to be, you know, Hook's ship as you're going through.

00:26:01.200 --> 00:26:05.400
Really, what I'm saying is that the Famicom version is Kingdom Hearts, and the North American

00:26:05.400 --> 00:26:12.240
version is just some, you know, early version of, like, how anime wasn't cool yet in America.

00:26:12.240 --> 00:26:13.740
So they just said, screw all that.

00:26:13.880 --> 00:26:17.400
We'll just make some nonsensical story that kids will appreciate instead.

00:26:17.400 --> 00:26:18.980
Anime still isn't cool.

00:26:19.720 --> 00:26:21.660
Oh, okay. Yeah.

00:26:22.640 --> 00:26:28.520
So the Famicom version had all the Alice in Wonderland enemies, but going back to that deep

00:26:28.520 --> 00:26:33.980
lore Joe was talking about, the American version features enemies from The Jungle Book, Country

00:26:33.980 --> 00:26:38.940
Bear Jamboree, Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs, Sleeping Beauty.

00:26:39.180 --> 00:26:44.160
So yeah, they just chose movies that we wouldn't necessarily be looking for those types of enemies.

00:26:49.000 --> 00:26:53.400
We can't talk about how the game sucks until we talk about the boss battles.

00:26:53.680 --> 00:26:55.560
Hmm. Let's hear it.

00:26:55.960 --> 00:26:59.660
Because that is just a very strange idea.

00:26:59.940 --> 00:27:05.560
I think they kind of, like, they saw what happened when you have the fire flower in Super

00:27:05.560 --> 00:27:11.100
Mario Brothers when you're going up against Bowser, but they forgot that, like, most players won't

00:27:11.100 --> 00:27:12.560
shoot Bowser to death.

00:27:12.700 --> 00:27:15.280
They'll just jump over him and land on the axe.

00:27:15.940 --> 00:27:17.200
See, I never did that.

00:27:18.780 --> 00:27:20.360
You're talking to the wrong guy, Mike.

00:27:21.140 --> 00:27:26.020
Got it. Well, you see, what I'm saying is, is, like, this game takes those same elements of, like,

00:27:26.120 --> 00:27:29.440
bosses that will shoot projectiles like crazy, keep in mind.

00:27:30.040 --> 00:27:35.820
And you have to, you know, be on the same platform as them and just kind of trade off shots with them.

00:27:35.820 --> 00:27:47.040
But they shoot so much faster than Mickey can shoot that if you're not trying, if you're not, like, being really good at dodging and also, like, kind of reverting back to if it's on platforms, like, going lower.

00:27:47.040 --> 00:27:51.280
If you're not doing that kind of stuff, you could die very fast.

00:27:51.280 --> 00:27:57.820
But if you do that kind of stuff, you could be in for, like, a five-minute boss battle with some of these bosses, right?

00:27:57.820 --> 00:28:15.400
Yeah, the alligator one specifically with just – it's just a barrage of projectiles moving on different trajectories so that you really – I can't think of a way that I would be able to get it without getting hit.

00:28:17.100 --> 00:28:25.760
Because they're not – there isn't, like, a designated safe area, like, if you jump in between them, because there's always another wave behind it.

00:28:25.900 --> 00:28:27.280
So, yeah, I agree.

00:28:27.500 --> 00:28:33.080
The way that they designed the bosses here, it's just sloppy.

00:28:33.800 --> 00:28:44.920
Yeah, and going back to, like, the lack of intent and things, it does feel like that there are sometimes just, like, that the projectiles are just so randomly placed that I think they could be wrong.

00:28:44.920 --> 00:28:49.320
But I think that there are times that you literally can't – it's literally impossible to avoid hits.

00:28:49.800 --> 00:28:52.280
Maybe I'm wrong, but it sure felt like that.

00:28:53.280 --> 00:29:08.420
And then on the other side of it, in the first boss battle against the witch, I don't know how you would beat it other than – I mean, how you would not get hit other than the way that I eventually learned you could do, which is climb up the ladder as Mickey and Minnie.

00:29:08.420 --> 00:29:12.220
Climb down the ladder, but as soon as you step off, move to the side as Mickey.

00:29:12.460 --> 00:29:13.780
So, Minnie is stuck on the ladder.

00:29:14.280 --> 00:29:15.740
Climb back up the ladder as Minnie.

00:29:15.940 --> 00:29:17.400
She's up there. She can't get hit.

00:29:17.720 --> 00:29:18.560
Mickey's down below.

00:29:18.560 --> 00:29:20.520
He's safe. And you just fire away.

00:29:20.700 --> 00:29:23.020
Wow, you've got – you've got this whole thing going.

00:29:23.700 --> 00:29:27.480
Yeah, this is, like – this is, like, for speed running the game.

00:29:27.560 --> 00:29:29.180
This is the game FAQ version.

00:29:29.460 --> 00:29:47.840
Yeah. I say use every mechanic the game gives you to your advantage, but I can't help feel that these boss fights are, like – you know how, like, when you're playing some JRPGs or just – or just, you know, tough bosses in general, they usually have, like, one devastating move that you have to watch out for.

00:29:48.060 --> 00:29:55.640
But thankfully, it just happens, like – it happens for, like, a short instance or it's an attack that does, like, a lot of damage, but they only do it once in a fight.

00:29:55.640 --> 00:29:58.720
Like, I feel like that's just what these bosses are doing at all times.

00:29:58.820 --> 00:30:04.160
Like, they start with their final move, and they just keep unleashing it until you die.

00:30:04.680 --> 00:30:06.540
Just supernova over and over.

00:30:07.060 --> 00:30:09.080
Right. Okay.

00:30:09.640 --> 00:30:11.660
Any other thoughts on the game from you guys?

00:30:12.200 --> 00:30:21.580
Not really. It's kind of sad how much lack of intent and, like, weird enemy designs kind of sums up the game.

00:30:21.840 --> 00:30:23.780
I guess I get one thing.

00:30:23.780 --> 00:30:33.540
I did appreciate that each level played kind of differently, even though that execution left something to be desired.

00:30:33.540 --> 00:30:51.380
But the fact that you're going from, like, a maze of a house and then a more stereotypical platforming experience in the second level and then a wood maze after that.

00:30:51.500 --> 00:30:54.900
Like, I appreciate that it switched up its gameplay loop a little bit.

00:30:55.980 --> 00:31:06.660
Yeah, I think what we have here is a game that tried to, like, take lessons from good video games and at some points applies them correctly.

00:31:06.740 --> 00:31:10.260
But the overall execution just kind of stumbles.

00:31:10.560 --> 00:31:17.120
And I think of things even, like, when you jump, you can change the momentum of your jump.

00:31:17.120 --> 00:31:22.160
Like, a long press will jump you higher than a short press, which is great.

00:31:22.320 --> 00:31:26.180
You know, like, that's what we look for in good jumping mechanics in NES games.

00:31:26.380 --> 00:31:35.200
But if you jump forward and then you realize you're going to land into an enemy, if you try to move backwards, you just fall straight down.

00:31:35.520 --> 00:31:36.800
Or mini will, at least.

00:31:37.220 --> 00:31:47.100
Yeah. Yeah, but, like, you can't change, you know, you're not, like, so floaty and so in control that you can really platform in this game the way you want to.

00:31:47.300 --> 00:31:52.480
So I think, like, the ideas were here, but I don't know if they quite landed on them.

00:31:52.640 --> 00:31:55.100
I will say that, landed, platformer.

00:31:55.920 --> 00:32:00.580
I will say that to Capcom's defense, this was a Hudson Soft game.

00:32:00.580 --> 00:32:11.760
And probably Capcom learned some lessons from this and might have said, like, you know, hey, in the future we might have to develop these games ourselves if we want a relationship with Disney.

00:32:12.820 --> 00:32:14.020
That makes sense.

00:32:14.260 --> 00:32:16.000
Because, I mean, they did get better.

00:32:16.180 --> 00:32:21.420
Like, my experience with the Super NES games tells me that.

00:32:21.620 --> 00:32:26.900
They're not groundbreaking, but they're definitely got more spit and polish.

00:32:29.860 --> 00:32:37.140
There is one really cool thing about Mickey Mousecapade, though, that has nothing to do with the actual game.

00:32:37.320 --> 00:32:47.240
However, if you take the cartridge out of your NES and you unscrew it, they put a hidden Mickey on the actual circuit board.

00:32:47.440 --> 00:32:49.620
Are you guys familiar with the concept of hidden Mickeys?

00:32:50.280 --> 00:32:53.180
Yes. But that's, are you serious?

00:32:53.360 --> 00:32:54.560
Physically, there's a hidden Mickey there?

00:32:54.560 --> 00:33:02.240
There's a physically, it's a, you know, it's probably programmed to nothing, but it's, like, soldered on as the Mickey Mouse ears.

00:33:02.920 --> 00:33:03.780
That's cute.

00:33:04.260 --> 00:33:05.180
I like that.

00:33:05.380 --> 00:33:08.560
You know, like, Disney does that all over the theme parks.

00:33:08.940 --> 00:33:13.160
And I just thought it was, like, a really nice touch to also have that hidden on the game.

00:33:13.220 --> 00:33:17.900
I wonder whose decision that was since no consumer would ever really see it.

00:33:17.900 --> 00:33:24.780
It was probably just some guy that was in the, that actually worked for the company that manufactured the cartridges.

00:33:25.880 --> 00:33:26.720
I don't know.

00:33:27.200 --> 00:33:28.060
No, no, it's true.

00:33:28.120 --> 00:33:30.560
It's true. It's probably, it is probably something like that.

00:33:30.560 --> 00:33:32.620
Because you're not supposed to see that. Right, right.

00:33:32.720 --> 00:33:40.400
Yeah. I mean, I'm sure in the NES manual, amongst other things, like, don't sit on your cartridges, I'm sure that it also says don't open them.

00:33:40.480 --> 00:33:42.480
Or you'll, like, void warranty, you know?

00:33:42.480 --> 00:33:46.600
Yeah. So, that's just, that's just a cool thing, I thought.

00:33:46.920 --> 00:34:00.120
And then, in the realm of not cool things, IGN put this as the 86th greatest NES game of all time on their list of the 100 greatest NES games.

00:34:00.580 --> 00:34:04.280
And we've played, I don't know how long ago, to be honest.

00:34:04.500 --> 00:34:10.820
I should, it's probably old now, and they probably, like, know, because people probably call them out about this.

00:34:10.820 --> 00:34:17.380
But, I would just say that, like, we've played 100 and, almost 130 NES games.

00:34:18.760 --> 00:34:20.200
And, this would fall into.

00:34:20.200 --> 00:34:21.760
It's already lower than 86. Right, right, right.

00:34:22.300 --> 00:34:32.480
This, yeah, this would fall maybe into our top 100 right now, but has, like, zero chance of keeping, of staying with that momentum as we get closer to the other 700.

00:34:32.800 --> 00:34:33.980
Yeah, I have to agree.

00:34:34.220 --> 00:34:37.420
Otherwise, we're going to have a very bad time for the rest of this podcast.

00:34:38.000 --> 00:34:44.600
Yeah. Okay, so look, there are so many Mickey Mouse video games, and there are so many Capcom Disney video games,

00:34:44.640 --> 00:34:48.980
that we are not going to do an extensive sequels and spinoff section here.

00:34:49.660 --> 00:34:56.000
We can talk about the games that we'll be seeing between Capcom and Disney on the NES that we haven't played yet,

00:34:56.000 --> 00:35:06.880
such as Avengers in the Magic Kingdom, Darkwing Duck, DuckTales 1 and 2, The Little Mermaid, Tailspin,

00:35:07.580 --> 00:35:12.160
and Chip and Dale Rescue Rangers 1 and 2, which is very personal to me and Joe.

00:35:13.040 --> 00:35:14.440
Well, half of it is.

00:35:14.660 --> 00:35:15.640
You know, all the stuff with Dale.

00:35:16.680 --> 00:35:18.320
Just wait until you get to Dale.

00:35:18.740 --> 00:35:20.360
Yeah. So much better than Chip.

00:35:21.160 --> 00:35:23.120
Sean, are you excited for any of those?

00:35:23.820 --> 00:35:30.620
The only – is the Adventures in the Magic Kingdom the one where you're kind of – like, you have, like, a world map that's just Disneyland?

00:35:30.940 --> 00:35:34.160
You know, I don't actually know if you get a world map or not in that game.

00:35:34.200 --> 00:35:36.900
